16
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:37,160
Linked list is an arrangement of

17
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:38,930
values where every value
is going to be paired

18
00:00:38,930 --> 00:00:40,640
with a pointer to the next and

19
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:42,050
possibly the previous value

20
00:00:42,050 --> 00:00:44,255
depending on how the
linked list is setup.

21
00:00:44,255 --> 00:00:46,700
It's very similar to an array in

22
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:49,480
terms of the
functionality and use,

23
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:50,990
but the difference
is that there's

24
00:00:50,990 --> 00:00:53,735
no requirement to have
sequential storage locations.

25
00:00:53,735 --> 00:00:55,610
Whereas in array you have

26
00:00:55,610 --> 00:00:59,665
one item located directly
after each another in memory,

27
00:00:59,665 --> 00:01:02,840
with the link list
implementation,

28
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:04,550
you can locate these
anywhere and you just

29
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:07,255
use a pointer to point
to the next element.

30
00:01:07,255 --> 00:01:10,580
Some benefits of linked
lists over arrays

31
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:13,480
is that you don't need to
know the size in advance,

32
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:15,290
because you can allocate

33
00:01:15,290 --> 00:01:18,170
arbitrary linked list node items

34
00:01:18,170 --> 00:01:20,360
later and then just
create links to them.

35
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:22,670
You don't have to reserve
some chunk of memory

36
00:01:22,670 --> 00:01:25,460
for the structure that

37
00:01:25,460 --> 00:01:27,350
you want to put it
in a linked list.

38
00:01:27,350 --> 00:01:29,480
It also means that the
size of the list is

39
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:31,370
only implemented by the
size of the memory.

40
00:01:31,370 --> 00:01:32,570
As long as you can allocate

41
00:01:32,570 --> 00:01:35,255
memory associated with the node,

42
00:01:35,255 --> 00:01:38,020
then you can add a node
to the linked list.

43
00:01:38,020 --> 00:01:40,700
Another benefit is that you
can add or remove nodes to

44
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:41,810
the sequence without moving

45
00:01:41,810 --> 00:01:43,890
memory. What do we mean by this?

46
00:01:43,890 --> 00:01:45,990
If you're going to remove one

47
00:01:45,990 --> 00:01:47,250
of the items from
the linked list,

48
00:01:47,250 --> 00:01:48,915
you can just change the pointer,

49
00:01:48,915 --> 00:01:50,750
instead of pointing
to item number 1,

50
00:01:50,750 --> 00:01:52,835
we point to item number 2 and

51
00:01:52,835 --> 00:01:54,995
that item will be removed
from the linked list.

52
00:01:54,995 --> 00:01:56,845
We didn't have to copy anything,

53
00:01:56,845 --> 00:01:58,260
we didn't have to copy two to

54
00:01:58,260 --> 00:02:00,765
a different location
for instance.

55
00:02:00,765 --> 00:02:03,225
Some of the drawbacks of

56
00:02:03,225 --> 00:02:05,310
linked lists compared to arrays,

57
00:02:05,310 --> 00:02:07,610
there's an extra memory usage

58
00:02:07,610 --> 00:02:09,590
for the pointer to
the next element,

59
00:02:09,590 --> 00:02:12,110
because we're storing
more than just a value,

60
00:02:12,110 --> 00:02:13,565
we're also storing a pointer.

61
00:02:13,565 --> 00:02:16,405
Every one of these pointers

62
00:02:16,405 --> 00:02:19,575
if you had a large enough list,

63
00:02:19,575 --> 00:02:20,930
then that would add up to a

64
00:02:20,930 --> 00:02:23,075
significant amount of
additional memory.

65
00:02:23,075 --> 00:02:24,950
Then also indexing.

66
00:02:24,950 --> 00:02:28,885
If you just did array
value index by offset,

67
00:02:28,885 --> 00:02:33,740
that's essentially a
single cycle instruction

68
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:35,975
or the equivalent of

69
00:02:35,975 --> 00:02:38,825
a very quick instruction
on an array.

70
00:02:38,825 --> 00:02:42,300
But for a linked list it's

71
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:44,105
order n. The reason is

72
00:02:44,105 --> 00:02:46,295
you're going to have to
iterate over multiple.

73
00:02:46,295 --> 00:02:48,575
If I wanted to find
out what number 2 is,

74
00:02:48,575 --> 00:02:50,840
I don't know what that
memory location is

75
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:53,540
until I follow the links from

76
00:02:53,540 --> 00:02:56,060
the head pointer and
go through each one of

77
00:02:56,060 --> 00:02:57,950
the items to find the entry

78
00:02:57,950 --> 00:03:00,840
for that particular
item I'm looking for.

79
00:03:01,060 --> 00:03:03,230
There's a couple of
different options for

80
00:03:03,230 --> 00:03:05,530
implementing a linked list.

81
00:03:05,530 --> 00:03:09,260
Option number 1 is you can
implement yourself and maybe

82
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:10,700
you would like to get

83
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:13,265
more experience
implementing a linked list,

84
00:03:13,265 --> 00:03:16,010
or you have a specific way
that you'd like to do it,

85
00:03:16,010 --> 00:03:17,020
that's a fine way,

86
00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:19,300
you can do that for this
assignment if you like.

87
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:20,570
If you are going to do that,

88
00:03:20,570 --> 00:03:22,460
there are a couple steps

89
00:03:22,460 --> 00:03:23,980
that require special
consideration,

90
00:03:23,980 --> 00:03:25,160
so you definitely
need to think through

91
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:26,795
this before you
implement your own.

92
00:03:26,795 --> 00:03:29,690
One is removing the first
element or the head,

93
00:03:29,690 --> 00:03:31,790
because typically then
you've got to change

94
00:03:31,790 --> 00:03:33,890
some other external
pointer that points to

95
00:03:33,890 --> 00:03:36,295
the beginning of
your linked list.

96
00:03:36,295 --> 00:03:38,300
Also you need to
think about removing

97
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:39,875
elements while iterating.

98
00:03:39,875 --> 00:03:41,630
If you're iterating over
a list and then you're

99
00:03:41,630 --> 00:03:43,600
removing one of the
elements in your list,

100
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:45,380
you have to be careful
about making sure

101
00:03:45,380 --> 00:03:47,000
that all your pointers
are synchronized such

102
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:48,110
that you know that
you're going to

103
00:03:48,110 --> 00:03:49,730
the next element correctly

104
00:03:49,730 --> 00:03:52,200
after you've removed an element.

105
00:03:52,250 --> 00:03:54,410
Option number 2 is that you can

106
00:03:54,410 --> 00:03:56,420
use an existing implementation.

107
00:03:56,420 --> 00:03:57,530
One benefit of that is

108
00:03:57,530 --> 00:03:58,550
that it's probably
going to be more

109
00:03:58,550 --> 00:04:00,050
thoroughly tested than

110
00:04:00,050 --> 00:04:01,915
your implementation
is going to be.

111
00:04:01,915 --> 00:04:03,920
Theoretically,
it's going to save

112
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:06,770
time depending on how fast
you'd write it yourself,

113
00:04:06,770 --> 00:04:08,995
but it's probably going
to be a time-saver.

114
00:04:08,995 --> 00:04:10,970
You could also get familiar with

115
00:04:10,970 --> 00:04:13,010
using different APIs
and getting used

116
00:04:13,010 --> 00:04:15,320
to reading an API spec

117
00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:17,570
and implementing based
on someone else's spec,

118
00:04:17,570 --> 00:04:21,520
which is a useful skill to
have as a software developer.

119
00:04:21,520 --> 00:04:24,620
I recommend using option
number 2 unless there's

120
00:04:24,620 --> 00:04:26,450
a specific reason you
