190
00:07:23,010 --> 00:07:26,275
Another thing to talk about in

191
00:07:26,275 --> 00:07:29,185
assignment 6 is the
requirement to add threading.

192
00:07:29,185 --> 00:07:31,900
Part of what we'll be doing
in assignment 6 is add

193
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:33,790
threads to the socket
server implementation

194
00:07:33,790 --> 00:07:35,500
we did for assignment 5.

195
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:37,690
Every socket connection
now is going

196
00:07:37,690 --> 00:07:39,430
to start a new thread after

197
00:07:39,430 --> 00:07:40,990
the accept that's
going to handle

198
00:07:40,990 --> 00:07:43,510
data transfers to
and from the socket.

199
00:07:43,510 --> 00:07:46,375
How many threads
should you support?

200
00:07:46,375 --> 00:07:48,820
The requirement
says as many as you

201
00:07:48,820 --> 00:07:50,305
can with the memory you have.

202
00:07:50,305 --> 00:07:52,780
In other words, it's a good
application to use for

203
00:07:52,780 --> 00:07:54,100
a linked list because
we don't know

204
00:07:54,100 --> 00:07:55,870
how many we have to support,

205
00:07:55,870 --> 00:07:57,640
we don't have a
specific fixed size.

206
00:07:57,640 --> 00:07:59,785
We can't really allocate
a block of memory

207
00:07:59,785 --> 00:08:01,300
for thread structures and

208
00:08:01,300 --> 00:08:03,265
know that we'll have
enough to support it.

209
00:08:03,265 --> 00:08:04,840
It's part of the reason
of what we'll use

210
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:07,975
a linked list for
this assignment.

211
00:08:07,975 --> 00:08:11,620
You need to track the thread
IDs in your main process

212
00:08:11,620 --> 00:08:14,545
because you have to be able
to check for completion.

213
00:08:14,545 --> 00:08:16,360
For this I just
recommend you include

214
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:18,355
a simple variable
like a Boolean,

215
00:08:18,355 --> 00:08:20,650
like it's set by your thread.

216
00:08:20,650 --> 00:08:22,990
You can do this
every time you start

217
00:08:22,990 --> 00:08:25,269
a new thread in
the main process,

218
00:08:25,269 --> 00:08:28,255
you can just check to see
if all your threads are

219
00:08:28,255 --> 00:08:33,735
still running or if
any have completed.

220
00:08:33,735 --> 00:08:35,340
If any of the threats
have completed,

221
00:08:35,340 --> 00:08:37,935
you can use pthread_join
to join the thread ID.

222
00:08:37,935 --> 00:08:39,000
This is going to free

223
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:40,410
all the memory that
were associated with

224
00:08:40,410 --> 00:08:43,250
the thread and ensure that
you don't have memory leaks.

225
00:08:43,250 --> 00:08:45,820
Here's a flow chart that shows

226
00:08:45,820 --> 00:08:48,310
you how I suggest
that you implement.

227
00:08:48,310 --> 00:08:49,705
You don't have to use this,

228
00:08:49,705 --> 00:08:52,270
but if you're looking for a
suggestion about how you can

229
00:08:52,270 --> 00:08:54,010
implement the threading required

230
00:08:54,010 --> 00:08:55,405
for assignment number 6,

231
00:08:55,405 --> 00:08:57,670
you can inside your
main process thread,

232
00:08:57,670 --> 00:08:59,590
you accept the socket connection

233
00:08:59,590 --> 00:09:01,210
and then you would
create a thread,

234
00:09:01,210 --> 00:09:03,085
and you would add that
thread to the list.

235
00:09:03,085 --> 00:09:05,425
Then you'd say for every
thread that's in the list,

236
00:09:05,425 --> 00:09:07,270
is the thread complete flag set?

237
00:09:07,270 --> 00:09:08,845
If the answer was no,

238
00:09:08,845 --> 00:09:11,650
you'd go back in

239
00:09:11,650 --> 00:09:15,055
and wait for a new connection
here in accept connection.

240
00:09:15,055 --> 00:09:17,515
If the answer was yes,
you'd pthread_join

241
00:09:17,515 --> 00:09:19,450
the thread complete for

242
00:09:19,450 --> 00:09:20,530
any threads that were complete

243
00:09:20,530 --> 00:09:21,865
and free that associate memory.

244
00:09:21,865 --> 00:09:24,760
Then you go back and wait
for new connections.

245
00:09:24,760 --> 00:09:26,920
Then you're sending
received threads are

246
00:09:26,920 --> 00:09:29,320
just as simple as receive and

247
00:09:29,320 --> 00:09:31,195
send on the socket

248
00:09:31,195 --> 00:09:34,540
based on the requirements
of assignment 5.

249
00:09:34,540 --> 00:09:36,940
Then set a complete flag to let

250
00:09:36,940 --> 00:09:39,700
this main process
thread know that you've

251
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:42,100
completed your work
and exit the thread so

252
00:09:42,100 --> 00:09:45,745
that a pthread_join would
complete successfully.

253
00:09:45,745 --> 00:09:49,120
A couple of pitfalls to avoid
with threading based on

254
00:09:49,120 --> 00:09:50,380
problems students have had

255
00:09:50,380 --> 00:09:51,940
with this assignment
in the past,

256
00:09:51,940 --> 00:09:55,225
try to deallocate memory
only in one place.

257
00:09:55,225 --> 00:09:57,160
Usually the best
place to do that

258
00:09:57,160 --> 00:09:59,485
is within the main
process thread.

259
00:09:59,485 --> 00:10:01,840
You can think about
doing it different ways,

260
00:10:01,840 --> 00:10:03,190
there might be other
ways to do it,

261
00:10:03,190 --> 00:10:05,470
but I suggest trying to
make sure that you're

262
00:10:05,470 --> 00:10:08,260
only deallocating
memory in one place.

263
00:10:08,260 --> 00:10:11,140
Keep in mind that the
linked list implementation

264
00:10:11,140 --> 00:10:12,835
itself is not thread safe.

265
00:10:12,835 --> 00:10:14,215
It's best not to try to

266
00:10:14,215 --> 00:10:16,375
manipulate the list from
two different threads.

267
00:10:16,375 --> 00:10:17,560
If you want to do that, you can,

268
00:10:17,560 --> 00:10:18,970
but you need to add
your own locking in

269
00:10:18,970 --> 00:10:20,830
order to do that safely.

270
00:10:20,830 --> 00:10:23,680
The easiest way to do this
is to just only access

271
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:26,635
the link list itself from
the main process thread,

272
00:10:26,635 --> 00:10:27,850
which you can do using

273
00:10:27,850 --> 00:10:30,715
the flowchart that I showed
you on the previous slide.

274
00:10:30,715 --> 00:10:32,995
Don't forget to pthread_join

275
00:10:32,995 --> 00:10:34,390
because if you do forget that,

276
00:10:34,390 --> 00:10:37,075
you'll see an error message
come out of algorithm

277
00:10:37,075 --> 00:10:41,420
that tells you that you've
some [inaudible] free.

278
00:10:41,610 --> 00:10:43,840
Question that's
come from the past;

279
00:10:43,840 --> 00:10:46,000
why don't I just use
detached threads then I

280
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,640
don't have to worry about
doing pthread_join?

281
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:50,860
You can read more
about that, there's

282
00:10:50,860 --> 00:10:51,970
a Stack Overflow posts that

283
00:10:51,970 --> 00:10:53,635
goes through this
in a lot of detail.

284
00:10:53,635 --> 00:10:55,270
But it's really only safe to

285
00:10:55,270 --> 00:10:56,740
use detached threads
when you have

286
00:10:56,740 --> 00:10:59,410
some other synchronization
method that you can use to

287
00:10:59,410 --> 00:11:02,890
know for a fact that the
thread is terminated.

288
00:11:02,890 --> 00:11:06,445
It can introduce some race
conditions on shutdown.

289
00:11:06,445 --> 00:11:08,170
I suggest that you don't try to

290
00:11:08,170 --> 00:11:10,255
use detached threads
and just use

291
00:11:10,255 --> 00:11:13,210
the pthread_join strategy and

292
00:11:13,210 --> 00:11:14,950
a linked list to track
the threads like

293
00:11:14,950 --> 00:11:17,660
the one that we've
discussed here.